This package provides library(typedef), a small library for
processing type declarations.

See library(typedef) module header for more information.

---++++ Quick test

This is a small test file that you can use to check if it works.
==
:- use_module(library(typedef)).

:- type maybe(A) ---> nothing; just(A).
:- type natural == nonneg.

%% test(+A:natural, +B:maybe(natural)) is det.
test(A,B) :-
	must_be(natural,A),
	must_be(maybe(natural),B).
==

As of version 0.2.0, =|typedef|= supports generalised algebraic data
types, where a parameterised type can have individually declared
constructors with different instantiations of the type parameter,
eg things like this:
==
:- type expr(_).
:- type int(integer) :: expr(integer).
:- type fl(float) :: expr(float).
:- type expr(A) + expr(A) :: expr(A).
==
See the file =|test/example.pl|= for more examples.
